          "type": "text",
          "text": "I've now loaded all the context. I can see several critical tensions between the documentation, the plan, and the actual code. Let me start the grilling session — one question at a time.\n\n---\n\n## Grill-with-Docs: Issue #3 — Triage Interview\n\nI've loaded all context files and explored both codebases. Let me walk down the design tree, resolving one branch at a time.\n\n---\n\n**Question 1: Entity Model — Single `TriageSubmission` vs Separate Entities**\n\nYour `CONTEXT.md` glossary is crystal clear:\n\n> *Triage Submission: A complete symptom report submitted by a User for AI-assisted analysis. Contains the full conversation history (initial complaint + AI follow-up questions + User answers) and the final AI triage result. **A single entity — no separate TriageResult.** *\n\nAnd the frontend types already encode this — `TriageSubmissionResource.attributes` contains `conversationHistory`, `specialist`, `urgency`, `justification` all in one object.\n\nBut `backend/agents.md` (the architecture spec) still shows a separate `TriageResult.php` entity (line 53) with its own directory. And the handoff doc references it as a separate concern.\n\n**My recommendation**: Follow `CONTEXT.md` + the frontend types — single `TriageSubmission` entity with a JSON column for `conversationHistory` and nullable fields for the final result (`specialist`, `urgency`, `justification` — all null until the AI produces a result). The `backend/agents.md` spec should be treated as out-of-date on this point.\n\nDo you agree with this single-entity approach? If so, I'll update `backend/agents.md` to reflect the correct model.",
