          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is asking me to evaluate whether the single-entity approach is correct from a senior engineering perspective. This is a great question - let me think deeply about this.\n\nThe domain glossary says TriageSubmission is a single entity containing everything: conversation history + final result. No separate TriageResult. The question is whether this is the right pattern for a portfolio project at the senior level.\n\nLet me think about the trade-offs:\n\n**Arguments FOR single entity:**\n1. Simplicity - one table, one entity, one repository. Less code, fewer migrations.\n2. The domain definition is clear: a Triage Submission IS the conversation + the result. You don't have a \"result\" independent of the \"submission\" — a result without a submission doesn't make sense.\n3. It's a portfolio project with a 2-week timeline — pragmatism wins.\n4. The conversation history is inherently part of the submission — you can't have a triage result without the conversation that led to it.\n5. Avoids N+1 query issues — everything is fetched in one query.\n6. The `frontend/types.ts` already models it this way — consistency with the API contract.\n\n**Arguments AGAINST single entity:**\n1. JSON column for conversation history is not queryable by the database. If you ever need to search conversation messages, you can't do it efficiently.\n2. If the conversation history grows large (though it's capped at ~3000 chars by the character limits), it's stored in one column, making row size potentially large.\n3. The `specialist`, `urgency`, `justification` fields are null until the AI completes — this is technically OK but can be seen as mixing states.\n\n**Arguments for separate TriageResult:**\n1. Clear separation of concerns — the conversation is one thing, the analysis result is another.\n2. The result is only meaningful after processing is complete — having it as a separate entity enforces the lifecycle.\n3. Could theoretically allow re-analysis or multiple results on the same submission.\n\n**Senior perspective judgment:**\n\nFor a 2-week portfolio project demonstrating Symfony + React + AI integration, the single-entity approach is actually the RIGHT call, but NOT just because it's simple. Here's why:\n\n1. **Domain-Driven Design principle**: An aggregate root should encapsulate its full lifecycle. TriageSubmission IS the aggregate — conversation + result. The result has no independent existence outside the submission.\n\n2. **This is a value object relationship, not an entity relationship**: The triage result doesn't have its own identity, lifecycle, or independent queries. It's a VALUE of the submission, not a separate ENTITY. In proper DDD, you'd model this as... a value embedded in the aggregate. A JSON column (or embeddable) is the correct pattern.\n\n3. **The frontend types got it right**: `TriageSubmissionResource` contains everything. The frontend team (or spec) naturally gravitated to the correct domain model because that's how you'd want to consume it — one API call, one response shape.\n\n4. **Performance**: A triage submission's conversation is always read together with the result. There's no scenario where you'd want just the result without the conversation, or just the conversation without the result. So there's no query reason to split them.\n\n5. **The character limits make it bounded**: With max 500 + 3×300 + 3×1000 = 4400 chars of conversation text, the JSON column will be tiny (a few KB at most). No blob concerns.\n\nSo my answer should be: **Yes, single entity is the correct pattern — not because it's simpler, but because the domain model (TriageResult has no independent identity, no independent lifecycle, and is always accessed together with its submission) means it should be a value within the TriageSubmission aggregate, not a separate entity.**\n\nThe one caveat I'd add is that `specialist`, `urgency`, `justification` should probably be a **Doctrine Embeddable** (a `TriageOutcome` value object) rather than loose nullable fields — that maintains the domain concept that these three fields form a cohesive unit that is either all present or all absent. But that's a refinement, not a rejection of the single-entity approach.\n\nLet me give this answer clearly and concisely.",
