          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user approved the single-entity + TriageOutcome embeddable approach. Let me proceed to the next question in the grilling session. The next major tension is the Turn/async flow design.\n\nThe core tension is:\n- CONTEXT.md describes an interactive, multi-turn conversation (Initial Symptom → up to 3 Turns of AI questions + user answers → final result or forced result at Turn 3)\n- The handoff doc says \"dispatches async Message\" via Messenger\n- But interactive multi-turn suggests synchronous (or at least the AI response per-turn should feel interactive)\n\nLet me think about the architectural options:\n\n1. **Fully async via Messenger**: Submit → queue → process → result. But with multi-turn, this doesn't work well for interactive follow-up questions.\n\n2. **Semi-sync per turn**: Initial submit creates the submission (pending), then each turn is a synchronous HTTP call to OpenRouter (blocking the request, but OpenRouter usually responds in 1-3 seconds for free models). The AI response determines: follow-up question OR final result.\n\n3. **Hybrid**: Submit is async, but turns are sync (since user is waiting).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The OpenRouter free models may be slower. But for a demo, a 2-3 second wait per turn is acceptable. Making it all async with polling would make the UX worse.\n\nLet me think about what actually happens:\n1. User types initial symptom → POST /api/triage/submit → backend creates TriageSubmission with status \"pending\", adds first message to conversationHistory, calls AI synchronously (or sends via Messenger), AI returns either a follow-up question or a final result, submission status changes to \"awaiting_answer\" or \"completed\"\n2. User sees AI follow-up question, types answer → POST /api/triage/{id}/answer → backend adds answer to conversationHistory, calls AI again with full history, AI returns either another question or final result\n3. Repeat up to 3 times, force final result on turn 3\n\nWait, but the handoff says \"ProcessTriageMessage — Messenger message for async AI processing\". The handoff doc's dependency graph also shows: Task 6 (entity) → Task 9 (AI service) → Task 10 (command/handler + message) → Task 11 (controller).\n\nBut looking more carefully: the conversation flow is inherently interactive! Each Turn requires:\n1. User message → API call\n2. Backend sends conversation history to AI\n3. AI responds (either question or result)\n4. Backend returns response to user\n\nIf we use Messenger for each AI call, we'd need polling between every turn, which makes the UX janky for a demo.\n\nHowever, I think the right approach for a \"portfolio demo\" is:\n- **Each Turn is synchronous** (PHP makes an HTTP call to OpenRouter, waits ~2-4 seconds, returns response)\n- **Messenger is used for Synthetic Cases only** (Issue #6, not #3)\n- The handoff's `ProcessTriageMessage` might be premature for Issue #3\n\nBut wait - the handoff very explicitly says \"dispatches async Message\" in the Submit flow. Let me re-read the handoff...\n\nThe handoff says:\n> SubmitTriageCommand + SubmitTriageHandler — creates submission, dispatches async Message\n> ProcessTriageMessage — Messenger message for async AI processing\n\nAnd the dependency graph shows Task 10 including the Message.\n\nHmm, but actual triage interview needs to be interactive. Let me look at the frontend types again:\n- `POST /api/triage/submit` → returns `{id, type, attributes: {status, submittedAt}}` - just creates the submission\n- `GET /api/triage/status/{id}` → returns `{status, currentTurn, lastAssistantMessage}` - polling for AI response\n- `POST /api/triage/{id}/answer` → returns `{status}` - user answers a question\n- `GET /api/triage/result/{id}` → final result\n\nSo the submit is async (creates submission, dispatches to Messenger, user polls status). But then the answer endpoint... does it also dispatch async? Or is it synchronous?\n\nActually, I think the flow might be:\n1. Submit (async) → AI generates first response (question or result) → user polls until status changes\n2. If question: user answers (sync? async?) → AI generates next response → user polls...\n\nThe problem is polling between every turn. For a demo, this could work - you submit, see a spinner for 5 seconds, see the AI's question, type your answer, see another spinner...\n\nNo wait, let me think about this differently. The real question is: **is this a chat-like experience (near-real-time) or a form-submission experience (submit and wait)?**\n\nFor a medical triage demo, a form-submission experience makes perfect sense:\n- \"Your case is being analyzed...\" (5-10 seconds)\n- \"The AI has a follow-up question: ...\" \n- User types answer, submits\n- \"Analyzing your response...\" (5-10 seconds)\n- Either another question or final result\n\nIn this model, Messenger-based async with polling is actually fine. The user doesn't expect instant chat replies - they expect thoughtful medical analysis.\n\nSo the design would be:\n1. `POST /api/triage/submit` → create TriageSubmission (pending) → dispatch `ProcessTriageMessage` → return 202 with submission ID\n2. Frontend polls `GET /api/triage/status/{id}` every 1-2 seconds\n3. When status changes from \"processing\" to \"awaiting_answer\" (AI returned a question) → show question\n4. `POST /api/triage/{id}/answer` → add answer, dispatch `ProcessTriageMessage` again → return 202\n5. Repeat polling until status is \"completed\" → redirect to result page\n\nThis makes architectural sense! The TriageSubmission entity needs:\n- `status`: pending → processing → awaiting_answer → processing → awaiting_answer → processing → completed | failed\n- `currentTurn`: which turn we're on (0 = initial, then 1, 2, 3)\n- `conversationHistory`: JSON array of messages\n\nOK so this is my next question. Let me formulate it cleanly.\n\nActually, I want to be really crisp here. The grilling question is about whether Messenger-based async with polling is the right approach for the interactive interview flow, or whether synchronous HTTP calls would be better for a demo.\n\nLet me ask this.",
