        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\n\n**Question 3: The Status State Machine — Is 4 states enough?**\n\nThe frontend types currently define: `pending | processing | completed | failed`\n\nBut a multi-turn conversation means the submission oscillates between \"waiting for user input\" and \"AI is thinking.\" Consider:\n\n```\nUser submits → pending → [Messenger picks up] → processing → AI responds with question\n```\n\nAt this point, is it still `processing`? The AI is done. The User needs to read the question and type an answer. Calling that `processing` is misleading — it's `awaiting_answer`.\n\nThen:\n\n```\nUser answers → [Messenger picks up] → processing → AI responds with another question\n```\n\nNow it's back to `awaiting_answer`. This could happen 1-3 times.\n\n**My recommendation**: Add `awaiting_answer` as a 5th state. The state machine becomes:\n\n```\npending → processing → awaiting_answer → processing → awaiting_answer → processing → completed\n           (failed)  ↑                                                              (failed)\n                     └──── (up to 2 more turns: user answer → Messenger picks up) ──┘\n```\n\nBenefits:\n- Frontend doesn't need to derive \"show the message + answer input\" from `lastAssistantMessage !== null` while `status === 'processing'` — the status itself says it\n- Admin dashboard can show exactly how many submissions are truly waiting on humans vs machines\n- Self-documenting API — a GET of the status tells a clear story\n\nThis requires updating the frontend `TriageStatusResource` type to include `'awaiting_answer'` in the union.\n\nAgree? If so, next question: the AI response discrimination problem — how do we know if the AI's response is \"here's another question\" vs \"here's the final triage result\"?",
