        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\n\n**Question 6: Turn Counting — Explicit Field or Derived?**\n\nThe `CONTEXT.md` defines Turn as a first-class concept:\n\n> *Turn: One complete exchange in the triage interview: a User message followed by the AI's response. The interview consists of the Initial Symptom Description plus up to 3 Turns — the AI can produce its final triage result in any Turn (1, 2, or 3).*\n\nThis creates a subtlety: the Initial Symptom Description is NOT Turn 1. It kicks things off, then the AI begins Turn 1.\n\nThe system needs to know the current turn to:\n1. Include it in the status response (`currentTurn` in frontend types)\n2. Determine when Turn = 3 → force a result response\n3. Include \"you are on Turn X of 3\" in the AI system prompt context\n\nTwo approaches:\n\n| Approach | How |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Explicit `$currentTurn: int` field** | Incremented when AI produces a follow_up (not on a result). Starts at 0 after submission. |\n| **B. Derived from conversation** | Count `type: follow_up` entries in conversation history JSON. `count(array_filter($history, fn($m) => $m['type'] === 'follow_up'))` |\n\n**My recommendation**: **Approach A** — explicit `$currentTurn` field. Derived is tempting (single source of truth) but fragile. The status endpoint (`GET /api/triage/status/{id}`) needs to return `currentTurn` — decoding and counting JSON on every poll is wasteful. An explicit field also makes the Turn-3 force logic dead simple: `if ($submission->getCurrentTurn() >= 3) { forceResult = true }`. And it's self-documenting in the DB schema.\n\nThe field starts at `0` after submission (no turns yet). Incremented when AI produces `type: follow_up`. Not incremented when AI produces `type: result` (submission completes instead).\n\nAgree?\n\n\n\n",
