        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\n\n**Question 7: Endpoints — Do the 5 Planned Ones Still Fit?**\n\nThe handoff doc and frontend `endpoints.ts` define:\n\n```\nPOST   /api/triage/submit          # Submit initial symptom\nPOST   /api/triage/{id}/answer     # User answers AI follow-up question\nGET    /api/triage/status/{id}     # Poll for status/next step\nGET    /api/triage/result/{id}     # Get final result\nGET    /api/triage/submissions     # List user's submissions\n```\n\nWith our 5-state machine, there's a tension in the flow. When the frontend polls `status` and gets `awaiting_answer`, it needs the AI's question to display. But `status` returns a lightweight payload. Then to submit an answer, the user calls `POST .../answer`. That's fine.\n\nBut `GET /api/triage/result/{id}` — once the status is `completed`, the frontend calls this to get `specialist`, `urgency`, `justification`, and the full conversation. This is read-only and could reasonably merge with:\n\n| Option | Endpoints |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Keep separate** | `status` returns lightweight (status + currentTurn + lastMessage), `result` returns full payload. 5 endpoints. |\n| **B. Merge into GET /api/triage/{id}** | Single endpoint returns everything (status, conversation, outcome — all nullable). Frontend derives display state from `status` + data shape. 4 endpoints. |\n\nOption A means `status` is cheap (no conversation decode) and `result` is heavy but called once. Option B is RESTier (single resource endpoint) but returns potentially big payloads on every poll.\n\n**My recommendation**: **Option A** — keep them separate. `status` is the polling endpoint (called frequently, lightweight). `result` is the detail endpoint (called once when done). This is the pattern already in the frontend types. The `status` response should also return the `lastAssistantMessage` so the frontend can show the AI's latest question while `awaiting_answer` without needing a full conversation fetch.\n\nAgree?\n\n\n\n",
