        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\n\n**Question 8: AI Analyzer — How Much Abstraction?**\n\nADR 0002 is clear: no `symfony/ai-bundle`, use `symfony/http-client` directly. The handoff wants a `TriageAnalyzer` service. But `backend/agents.md` is littered with `symfony/ai` examples (`PlatformInterface`, `Request`, `Response` objects) that no longer apply.\n\nThe OpenRouter API is OpenAI-compatible: `POST /api/v1/chat/completions` with a standard message array.\n\nTwo levels of abstraction:\n\n| Approach | Structure |\n|---|---|\n| **Minimal** | `TriageAnalyzer` calls `HttpClientInterface` directly. Reads `ai.openrouter.*` params from the container. One class, ~80 lines. |\n| **Wrapped** | `OpenRouterClient` wraps HTTP calls (API key header, base URL, error mapping). `TriageAnalyzer` depends on `OpenRouterClient`, focuses on prompt construction + response parsing. Two classes. |\n\nThe argument for a wrapper: future Issues (synthetic case generation in #6) will also call OpenRouter. A shared `OpenRouterClient` avoids duplicated HTTP setup. The argument against: it's 2 lines of header setup, and premature abstraction.\n\n**My recommendation**: **Wrapped** — one `OpenRouterClient` in `App\\Shared\\Infrastructure\\Ai\\` that wraps `HttpClientInterface` with the base URL + auth header. It returns decoded JSON or throws a typed `AiRequestFailedException`. Then `TriageAnalyzer` in `App\\Triage\\Application\\Service\\` depends on `OpenRouterClient` and focuses purely on triage logic (system prompt, message building, response discrimination). This keeps concerns separated: one class knows *how* to call OpenRouter, the other knows *what* to say.\n\nFuture synthetic generation (#6) just injects `OpenRouterClient` and writes its own prompt.\n\nAgree?\n\n\n\n",
