        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\n\n**Question 9: Frontend Interview Flow — State on the Page vs URL**\n\nThe triage interview is a multi-step process: type symptoms → submit → poll → see AI question → type answer → submit → poll → ... → see result. This could be a single page with internal state transitions, or multiple URL-driven pages.\n\n| Approach | UX |\n|---|---|\n| **Single page** (`/triage`) | All states (input, polling, answering, result) on one page. Internal state machine. No URL changes during the flow. |\n| **URL-driven** (`/triage` → `/triage/:id/status` → `/triage/:id/result`) | Each step navigates. Browser back button works naturally. Deep-link to a specific submission's status. |\n\nThe frontend routes already define both `/triage` and `/triage/:id/result` as separate pages. The handoff mentions \"TriagePage (symptom input + interview flow)\" — suggesting the interview flow itself lives on one page, with result navigation as a separate URL hit.\n\n**My recommendation**: **Single page** for the interview (`/triage`). After submission:\n- Poll status via `useQuery` with refetchInterval\n- When `status === 'awaiting_answer'`, show the AI's question + answer textarea\n- When `status === 'processing'`, show spinner\n- When `status === 'completed'`, `navigate('/triage/' + id + '/result')` to the separate result page\n\nThe result page (`/triage/:id/result`) fetches via `GET /api/triage/result/{id}` — just the final display. This means the result page is the only URL-driven step. The interview itself is a single-page state machine in `TriagePage`.\n\nAgree?\n\n\n\n",
