          "text": "---\n\n**Question 16: Test Strategy — What's the Testing Surface?**\n\nGiven TDD workflow is mandatory per the plan, let's define what gets tested at what level:\n\n**Backend tests:**\n\n| Layer | Test Type | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| `TriageOutcome` + `TriageStatus` | Unit | Enum values, embeddable construction |\n| `TriageSubmission` entity | Unit | Constructor, named factory, state transitions, turn increment |\n| `DoctrineTriageSubmissionRepository` | Integration | Save + findById, findByUser, status filtering |\n| `TriageSystemPrompt` | Unit | Template rendering at each turn, force-result variant at turn 3 |\n| `TriageAnalyzer` | Unit | Mock OpenRouterClient: happy path (question), happy path (result), malformed JSON, empty response, Turn 3 force |\n| `OpenRouterClient` | Unit | Mock HTTP client: headers, URL, error mapping |\n| `SubmitTriageHandler` | Integration | Creates submission, dispatches message |\n| `ProcessTriageMessage` handler | Integration | Processes pending → response, updates conversation |\n| `TriageController` | Functional (WebTestCase) | All 5 endpoints: submit → 202, answer → 202, status → ownership check, result → full payload |\n\n**Frontend tests:**\n- `useTriageInterview` hook — vitest with React Testing Library\n- Page components — render with mocked API, test state transitions\n- Component rendering — ConversationThread, UrgencyBadge, MessageBubble\n\nThat's roughly 12-15 test classes/methods minimum for 80%+ coverage. The `TriageAnalyzer` tests are the most important — they validate AI response parsing in isolation.\n\nDoes this coverage plan look right? And do you want vitest tests for the frontend or is tsc + eslint sufficient for a portfolio demo?",
