        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Let me summarize the current state and the blocking issue.\n\nThe current state is:\n- Tasks 01-04 (Foundation): All green - 61 tests\n- Tasks 05-07 (AI Layer): All green - 53 tests  \n- Tasks 08-09 (Pipeline handlers): Written but can't test because...\n- **BLOCKER**: The `services.yaml` references `%ai.openrouter.*%` parameters but `ai.yaml` config is not loading in the container. This prevents ALL container-based tests (Doctrine repositories, auth controllers, etc.) from running.\n\nThe root cause: `services.yaml` line 28-37 explicitly binds `OpenRouterClient` using `%ai.openrouter.base_url%` parameter references. But the `config/packages/ai.yaml` file's `parameters:` block is not being loaded by Symfony's container compiler. When the container tries to resolve `%ai.openrouter.base_url%`, it fails.\n\nThe fix: The `ai.yaml` file needs to be restructured. In Symfony, `config/packages/*.yaml` should define `parameters:` under the root, which IS what ai.yaml does. But the container compiler fails before reaching it because `services.yaml` is loaded first and fails when it encounters the unresolvable parameter.\n\nThe simplest fix: convert the `services.yaml` explicit binding to use service `bind` or `#[Autowire]` attributes on OpenRouterClient instead, so failures happen at autowiring time rather than compile time. OR: define the ai parameters in services.yaml directly instead of in a separate file.\n\nActually, the issue is question: why doesn't `config/packages/ai.yaml` load? Let me re-read the Symfony config loading logic...\n\nActually wait — `config/packages/ai.yaml` IS a standard Symfony config file. It should be loaded. Unless there's a file naming issue. Let me check: is `ai.yaml` being ignored because it starts with `a` and the loader has some issue?\n\nNo, that doesn't make sense. Let me try a different approach: move the parameters into `services.yaml` directly.",
