prod, the async transport would queue it — but for the answer endpoint, the user expects the AI to respond quickly, so maybe we should use the sync transport?\n\n   Hmm, the task spec says \"calls ProcessTriageMessageHandler synchronously\". I think this means dispatching to the bus synchronously — the controller doesn't call the handler directly; it dispatches a message and the bus processes it.\n\n   But wait — in dev/prod, the routing says `ProcessTriageMessage: async`, meaning it goes to the async transport (doctrine-based). The answer endpoint would dispatch to async, and the client would poll until the result is ready.\n\n   Actually, let me re-read the task spec more carefully:\n\n   \"POST /api/triage/{id}/answer: accepts {\"content\":\"...\"}, validates ownership, calls ProcessTriageMessageHandler synchronously, returns 200\"\n\n   \"Synchronously\" here means the answer endpoint should be a sync operation. But the handler is wired as `#[AsMessageHandler]`. For the functional test, the test env has `in-memory://` which processes synchronously.\n\n   I think the simplest approach is: the controller adds the answer, then dispatches ProcessTriageMessage to the bus. In test env, this processes synchronously. In dev, it goes to async (but the answer endpoint should still return a 202 Accepted or similar, not wait).\n\n   Actually, looking at the existing code — the `ProcessTriageMessageHandler` is registered as a message handler via `#[AsMessageHandler]` attribute. When a `ProcessTriageMessage` is dispatched, the bus calls this handler. The handler then loads the submission, extracts the last answer, calls the AI, and updates. \n\n   The controller flow for POST `/api/triage/{id}/answer`:\n   1. Load submission from DB\n   2. Check ownership (403 if not same user)\n   3. Add user answer via `$submission->addUserAnswer($content)`\n   4. Flush to persist the answer\n   5. Dispatch `ProcessTriageMessage` to message bus\n   6. The bus processes synchronously in test