e answer endpoint returns the status. The status after `addUserAnswer()` is `Processing`. The handler then processes and might change it to `AwaitingAnswer` or `Completed` or `Failed`. But due to async, the controller can't know the final result.\n\nActually, the flow from the grilled design says: \"initiate conversation (send to ai, get result), continue conversation (send answer, get ai response)\". The answer endpoint sends the user's answer and gets back the AI's response. This is synchronous — the user waits for the AI to respond.\n\nSo the answer endpoint should be synchronous. The controller should:\n1. Add the answer via `addUserAnswer()`\n2. Dispatch ProcessTriageMessage synchronously\n3. The handler processes the AI analysis\n4. Return the final status (which could be AwaitingAnswer, Completed, or Failed)\n\nTo make this synchronous, I can use the `sync://` transport. Or, I can just call the handler directly (bypass the bus) when handling the answer endpoint.\n\nHmm, but calling `#[AsMessageHandler]` classes directly breaks the Messenger pattern. Instead, I should inject the `MessageBusInterface` and dispatch to the bus. For synchronous processing, I should use the `sync` transport.\n\nWait, actually looking at the Messenger config:\n- `async` transport: `doctrine://default` (dev/prod)\n- `failed` transport: `doctrine://default?queue_name=failed`\n- `sync` transport: `sync://`\n\nThe routing is `ProcessTriageMessage: async`. So when dispatched, it goes to `async` transport. In test, `async` is overwritten to `in-memory://` which is synchronous.\n\nBut for dev/prod, `async` uses `doctrine://default` which is truly async. However, the answer endpoint is supposed to be synchronous. The submission handler dispatches `ProcessTriageMessage` after initial submit as async (the user doesn't wait for the follow-up question — they poll). But the answer endpoint sends a follow-up answer and gets the AI response synchronously.\n\nActually, I just realized: the initial submit is async because the user hasn't started interacting yet — they submit and then check back. But the follow-up answers are synchronous because the user is in a conversation — they send an answer and expect the AI response immediately.\n\nBut in production with real AI, this would be slow — the answer endpoint would block for several seconds while the AI processes. That's a problem.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. The grilled design says:\n- Submit: send to AI, record question OR result, return\n- Answer: send answer, get AI response, return (synchronous)\n\nThe frontend polling was for the STATUS polling endpoint — the frontend polls `GET /api/triage/status/{id}` to see if the status changed. But the answer endpoint returns the full AI response.\n\nWait no — looking at the task spec again: \"POST /api/triage/{id}/answer: calls ProcessTriageMessageHandler synchronously, returns 200 with {\"data\":{\"status\":\"...\"}}\"\n\nSo the answer endpoint runs the handler synchronously and returns the status. The handler processes the AI analysis and updates the submission. The response includes the status (which could be `awaiting_answer` or `completed` or `failed`).\n\nFor simplicity, I'll implement it as: controller adds answer + flushes, dispatches message to bus (which in test env is sync via in-memory://). The test env handles synchronously. For dev, we'll need to make it sync too — but that's a configuration issue, not a code issue. The controller code is the same regardless.\n\nActually, wait. For dev, the Messenger routing sends to `async` (doctrine://default), which requires running `bin/console messenger:consume`. The answer endpoint would return immediately before the handler runs. This is acceptable — the frontend polls for status.\n\nThe task spec says \"synchronously\" but in the test context, the `in-memory://` transport makes it synchronous automatically. In dev, it's async. The controller code doesn't need to change — it dispatches and returns.\n\nOK let me just implement this now. I have all the context I need.\n\nThe controller needs:\n1. `POST /api/triage/submit` — creates submission, runs SubmitTriageHandler, returns 202\n2. `POST /api/triage/{id}/answer` — adds answer, dispatches ProcessTriageMessage, returns 200\n\nLet me write the TriageController and the route config.",
