not terminal), skips Pending (it's AwaitingAnswer), then extracts last answer (finds none), returns.\n\n   Wait, but the handler in `ProcessTriageMessageHandler::__invoke()` has this check:\n   ```php\n   if ($submission->getStatus() === TriageStatus::Pending ||\n       $submission->getStatus() === TriageStatus::Completed ||\n       $submission->getStatus() === TriageStatus::Failed) {\n       return;\n   }\n   ```\n\n   No, the handler has separate checks. Let me re-read:\n   ```php\n   // No-op: already in a terminal state\n   if ($submission->getStatus() === TriageStatus::Completed\n       || $submission->getStatus() === TriageStatus::Failed) {\n       return;\n   }\n   \n   // No-op: Pending submission has only the initial description\n   if ($submission->getStatus() === TriageStatus::Pending) {\n       return;\n   }\n   ```\n\n   So for AwaitingAnswer status:\n   - Not terminal (not Completed/Failed) → continues\n   - Not Pending → continues\n   - Extracts last answer → finds none (conversation has [initial_description, question]) → returns null → no-ops\n\n   Then the status is still AwaitingAnswer. The answer endpoint should work.\n\n   Hmm, but the error says failed. Let me look at this more carefully. Maybe the issue is that `ProcessTriageMessageHandler` is being invoked via `#[AsMessageHandler]` on the async transport, but the transport implementation isn't working in test. The `in-memory://` transport should handle synchronously.\n\n   Wait, actually I think I know the issue. The `services.yaml` has:\n   ```php\n   App\\Shared\\Infrastructure\\Ai\\OpenRouterClient:\n       arguments:\n           ...\n   ```\n\n   This explicit service definition means the container has TWO services: the OpenRouterClient AND the mock we set. But since both are bound to the same interface (wait, OpenRouterClient implements OpenRouterClientInterface, not TriageAnalyzerInterface), this shouldn't conflict.\n\n   Let me think about this differently. The controller calls `SubmitT