resets the kernel. I need to use `$client->restart()` or manually craft requests.\n\nActually, the simplest fix: use a helper that creates an authenticated client and returns the email/token. Then use `self::ensureKernelShutdown()` between tests, and craft HTTP requests with the Bearer token manually.\n\nBetter approach: Use `KernelBrowser` directly with a custom method that handles auth without kernel re-boot.\n\nLet me rewrite the test to use a simpler pattern: the answer endpoint sends a request to an existing submission. The submission was created by user A. User B tries to answer and gets 403. Client A is already set up with the mock. Client B just makes a request to the answer endpoint of client A's submission.\n\nThe issue is creating TWO clients. In WebTestCase, you can:\n1. Call `static::createClient()` for the first client\n2. Do operations\n3. Call `self::ensureKernelShutdown()` \n4. Call `static::createClient()` again for the second client\n\nYes, that should work. Let me use this pattern.\n\nLet me also simplify the answer test — the \"failed\" status issue. Let me trace through exactly what happens:\n\n1. `testAnswerOnOwnedSubmissionReturns200`:\n   - `createAuthenticatedClient()` → registers user, gets token, creates client with auth header\n   - `mockAnalyzerWithQuestion()` → replaces TriageAnalyzerInterface with mock in container\n   - POST `/api/triage/submit` → SubmitTriageHandler runs:\n     - Validates description ✓\n     - Creates entity ✓\n     - Persists ✓\n     - Calls `$this->analyzer->analyzeInitial()` → mock returns question\n     - `$result['type'] === 'result'` → false → goes to else\n     - Adds question to entity: `$submission->addAiQuestion($result['content'])` → status = AwaitingAnswer\n     - Flush\n     - Dispatch `ProcessTriageMessage`\n     - ProcessTriageMessage handler runs (sync via in-memory://):\n       - Loads submission from DB (fresh copy)\n       - Status = AwaitingAnswer → not terminal, not Pending\n       - Extract last answer → conversation has [initial_description, question] → scans backward, finds no 'answer' type → returns null\n       - Returns (no-op)\n     - Returns submission\n   - Controller returns 202\n   - Test extracts submissionId\n\n   - POST `/api/triage/{id}/answer`:\n     - Controller loads submission\n     - Checks ownership ✓\n     - Status = AwaitingAnswer → check passes\n     - Calls `$submission->addUserAnswer('It hurts in the lower back.')` → status = Processing\n     - Saves\n     - Dispatches `ProcessTriageMessage`\n     - Handler runs:\n       - Loads submission (status = Processing) \n       - Not terminal, not Pending\n       - Extract last answer → finds 'answer' with content 'It hurts in the lower back.'\n       - Calls `$this->analyzer->analyzeFollowUp('It hurts in the lower back.', [...history...], currentTurn=1)`\n       - Mock returns `['type' => 'question', 'content' => 'Have you taken any medication?']`\n       - Result type is 'question' → `$submission->addAiQuestion('Have you taken any medication?')` → status = AwaitingAnswer\n       - Flush\n     - Controller then calls `$this->repository->save($submission)` — but `$submission` is the STALE entity from before the handler ran!\n     - This overwrites the handler's changes!\n     - Status gets set back to `Processing` (from addUserAnswer)\n\n   Wait, that would make the status `Processing`, not `Failed`. The error says \"failed\". So something else is going on.\n\n   Actually, maybe the problem is different. Let me check: when the controller calls `$this->repository->save($submission)` AFTER the handler has already saved the submission, the stale entity's state overwrites the handler's changes. The handler set status to AwaitingAnswer via addAiQuestion. But the controller's stale `$submission` still has status = Processing. So `save($submission)` persists the stale state.\n\n   This is a real bug in the controller. The fix: either reload the submission after dispatching, or don't call save again.\n\n   Actually, the better fix is: the controller should NOT call `repository->save()` again after the handler runs. The handler already flushes. The controller should reload the submission to get the latest state.\n\n   But that explains why the answer returns 200 with status \"Processing\" (not \"failed\"). The error says status is \"failed\" though.\n\n   Hmm, let me re-read the error carefully:\n   ```\n   Failed asserting that the Response status code is 200.\n   HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Content\n   {\"errors\":[{\"status\":\"422\",\"code\":\"INVALID_STATUS\",\"title\":\"Submission not awaiting answer\",\"detail\":\"Submission is in status \"failed\", expected \"awaiting_answer\".\"}]}\n   ```\n\n   So the submission status is \"failed\" when the answer endpoint is called. This means something marks the submission as failed BETWEEN the submit and the answer call.\n\n   Could the ProcessTriageMessage dispatch from the SubmitTriageHandler be the issue? The handler dispatches after addAiQuestion. The message handler loads the submission, but at that point it's AwaitingAnswer. The handler processes it, extracts last answer (finds none), returns. So status should remain AwaitingAnswer.\n\n   Unless... the handler encounters an error between extracting the answer and the return. Let me re-read the handler code:\n\n   ```php\n   $answerContent = $this->extractLastAnswer($submission->getConversationHistory());\n   if ($answerContent === null) {\n       // AwaitingAnswer with no answer yet (e.g., dispatched from initial submit)\n       return;\n   }\n\n   try {\n       $result = $this->analyzer->analyzeFollowUp(\n           $answerContent,\n           $submission->getConversationHistory(),\n           $submission->getCurrentTurn(),\n       );\n   } catch (TriageAnalysisFailedException) {\n       $submission->markFailed();\n       $this->entityManager->flush();\n       return;\n   }\n   ```\n\n   If `extractLastAnswer` returns non-null (which shouldn't happen since there's no answer), or if the analyzer throws... but the mock should return a question for analyzeFollowUp.\n\n   Wait — could the issue be that the handler for ProcessTriageMessage in the initial submit dispatch is processing BEFORE the submit handler has finished flushing? No — the controller calls `$handler($command)` which runs synchronously. Inside the handler:\n   1. Save entity\n   2. Call analyzer\n   3. addAiQuestion (status → AwaitingAnswer)\n   4. Flush\n   5. Dispatch ProcessTriageMessage → message handler processes\n\n   At step 4, the entity is flushed with status AwaitingAnswer. At step 5, the message handler loads a fresh copy from DB. Status is AwaitingAnswer. The handler should no-op.\n\n   Unless... the flush in step 4 doesn't actually commit to the DB because there's a transaction? In functional tests, each request runs within a transaction that's rolled back at the end. But within the same request, the flush should be visible.\n\n   Actually, let me check the issue from a different angle. Maybe the mock isn't being properly set up. The `static::getContainer()->set()` might not work as expected. Let me change the test to verify the mock is being used.\n\n   Actually, I think the most reliable approach is to use a test-specific service config. Let me create a `services_test.yaml` that overrides TriageAnalyzerInterface with a mock implementation.\n\n   Or even simpler: use `$client->getContainer()` instead of `static::getContainer()`.\n\n   Let me try a different debugging approach — run a single test with verbose error output.\n\n   Actually, let me just fix all three issues and retest:\n   1. Fix columnPrefix on embeddable (done)\n   2. Fix two-client tests (use ensureKernelShutdown)\n   3. Fix mock approach for functional tests (get container from client, not static)\n\nLet me rewrite the test file with these fixes.",
