        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The submission IS in \"failed\" status. But WHY? After submit, the handler sets status to AwaitingAnswer. Then dispatches ProcessTriageMessage. The handler runs and should no-op. But something is setting it to failed.\n\nLet me look at the test more carefully. The test flow is:\n1. `createAuthenticatedClient()` — registers user, logs in, gets JWT\n2. `mockAnalyzerWithQuestion($client)` — replaces TriageAnalyzerInterface with mock\n3. POST `/api/triage/submit` — creates submission\n4. `assertResponseStatusCodeSame(202)` — submit returns 202\n5. POST `/api/triage/{id}/answer` — tries to answer\n\nBetween steps 4 and 5, something marks the submission as \"failed\". But the submit returns 202, which means the handler ran successfully. If the handler marked it as failed, it would still return 202 (the handler doesn't throw on failure — it catches TriageAnalysisFailedException and marks as failed, returning 202).\n\nLet me check: the submit controller returns 202 regardless of whether the submission is completed or failed:\n```php\nreturn $this->json([\n    'data' => [\n        'id' => $submission->getId()->toRfc4122(),\n        'type' => 'triage_submission',\n        'attributes' => [\n            'status' => $submission->getStatus()->value,\n            'submittedAt' => $submission->getSubmittedAt()->format('c'),\n        ],\n    ],\n], Response::HTTP_ACCEPTED);\n```\n\nSo 202 means the request was accepted, regardless of the submission status. The status could be \"failed\" and the response would still be 202.\n\nSo the real question is: WHY does the SubmitTriageHandler mark the submission as failed?\n\nLooking at SubmitTriageHandler:\n```php\ntry {\n    $result = $this->analyzer->analyzeInitial($command->initialDescription);\n} catch (TriageAnalysisFailedException) {\n    $submission->markFailed();\n    $this->entityManager->flush();\n    return $submission;\n}\n```\n\nIf `analyzeInitial()` throws `TriageAnalysisFailedException`, the submission is marked as failed. But my mock returns `['type' => 'question', 'content' => '...']` for `analyzeInitial`. The mock shouldn't throw.\n\nUnless... the mock isn't actually being used! Maybe `$client->getContainer()->set()` doesn't work because the container is already compiled, or the TriageAnalyzer service is created BEFORE the mock is set.\n\nIn Symfony functional tests, the container is created once and shared across requests. When I call `$client->getContainer()->set(TriageAnalyzerInterface::class, $mock)`, this should replace the service. But if the service has already been resolved and cached, the replacement might not take effect.\n\nActually, in Symfony 7.4, `$container->set()` on a frozen container might not work. Services are resolved lazily, so if the TriageAnalyzer hasn't been instantiated yet, replacing it should work. But if ANY service that depends on TriageAnalyzerInterface has been resolved, the old instance is cached.\n\nThe SubmitTriageHandler depends on TriageAnalyzerInterface. When I call `$handler($command)`, the handler is resolved from the container, which resolves TriageAnalyzerInterface → creates the real TriageAnalyzer → tries to call OpenRouter → fails (no real API key) → throws TriageAnalysisFailedException → markFailed.\n\nThe fix: I need to set the mock BEFORE any request is made. But I'm setting it after creating the client. Let me check the timing:\n\n1. `createAuthenticatedClient()` → creates client, registers, logs in (these don't use TriageAnalyzer)\n2. `mockAnalyzerWithQuestion($client)` → sets mock on container\n3. `$client->jsonRequest('POST', '/api/triage/submit', ...)` → controller → handler → analyzer\n\nBetween steps 2 and 3, the TriageAnalyzerInterface service should be resolved for the first time. Since I set the mock in step 2, when step 3 resolves it, it should use the mock.\n\nBut wait — the container might not be mutable after boot. In Symfony 7.4, `ContainerBuilder::set()` is used for compile-time, and for the runtime container, `set()` might not work if the container is \"frozen\".\n\nActually, in Symfony `KernelBrowser`, the container is the test container. In `WebTestCase`, `static::getContainer()` and `$client->getContainer()` return the `test.service_container`, which is a special container that can be modified at runtime.\n\nBut the `test.service_container` delegates to the main container. If the main container has already resolved TriageAnalyzerInterface, setting it on the test container won't affect the main container's cached instance.\n\nSo maybe the `set()` is setting it on the test container, but when the controller resolves TriageAnalyzerInterface, it resolves from the MAIN container which has the real analyzer.\n\nThe correct way to mock services in functional tests with Symfony is to use `static::getContainer()->set()` which returns the test container. But this works differently in different Symfony versions.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach: use the `@test` environment override to replace the service.\n\nActually, the simplest fix: create a `config/services_test.yaml` that replaces TriageAnalyzerInterface with a test implementation. Or even simpler — create a test-only service configuration.\n\nOr: I could just modify the test to work with the real analyzer. But the real analyzer needs OpenRouter API key and makes real HTTP calls.\n\nActually, wait. Let me check: in the `.env.test`, I already have `OPENROUTER_API_KEY=test_key_not_real`. And in `phpunit.xml`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY=test_key`. So the real analyzer would try to call OpenRouter with a fake API key, get a 401/403, and throw.\n\nThe real fix: I need to ensure the mock is actually used. Let me try setting the mock BEFORE booting the kernel. I can do this by booting the kernel manually and then setting the mock.\n\nOr, simplest approach: add a test-specific service override in `config/services_test.yaml`.",
