        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is asking if there are any ADRs (Architecture Decision Records) that should be added to `docs/adr/` based on the Issue #3 work. Let me think about what decisions were made that meet the three criteria for an ADR:\n\n1. **Hard to reverse** — the cost of changing your mind later is meaningful\n2. **Surprising without context** — a future reader will wonder \"why did they do it this way?\"\n3. **The result of a real trade-off** — there were genuine alternatives and you picked one for specific reasons\n\nKey decisions from Issue #3:\n\n1. **Single-entity design for TriageSubmission** — TriageOutcome is an Embeddable, not a separate entity. This is a meaningful trade-off (simpler vs more flexible). Surprising: one might expect a separate entity for the outcome.\n\n2. **TriageAnalyzerInterface extracted for test mocking** — PHPUnit can't mock final classes. Multiple interfaces had to be extracted because Symfony encourages `final` classes. This is a pattern worth documenting — \"interface per service\" pattern. Surprising without context.\n\n3. **JSON column for conversation history** (vs separate Turn entity). Trade-off: simpler queries vs queryability of individual turns. For a demo, JSON column wins. Could be surprising.\n\n4. **Test double over mock for functional tests** — `TestTriageAnalyzer` is a static test double (not PHPUnit mock). Why? Because `$container->set()` doesn't work for services already in the container. This is surprising without context.\n\n5. **Doctrine Embeddable with nullable columns** — The embeddable has non-nullable types internally (`string`) but the ORM columns are `nullable: true` because the `Embedded` on the parent entity is nullable. This is a Doctrine limitation worth documenting.\n\nLet me evaluate each:\n\n**#1 Single-entity design**: This is a core domain decision. It's documented in CONTEXT.md already. But is it worth an ADR? It's surprising (you'd expect a separate TriageResult entity), hard to reverse (schema change), and had a trade-off (separate entity vs embedded). ✅ ADR candidate.\n\n**#2 Interface extraction pattern**: This is more of a project convention than an ADR. It's a pattern, not an architectural decision with alternatives. ❌ Skip.\n\n**#3 JSON column for conversation**: Has alternatives (separate Turn entity table). Trade-off: queryability vs simplicity. Surprising: yes (some DBAs would normalize). ✅ ADR candidate.\n\n**#4 Test double over mock**: This is a testing pattern, not really architecture-level. ❌ Skip.\n\n**#5 Doctrine Embeddable nullable columns**: This is a specific Doctrine technical detail, not a wide-ranging architectural decision. It's a workaround. ❌ Skip.\n\nSo I think two ADRs are worth adding:\n- ADR-0004: Single-entity TriageSubmission with Embeddable TriageOutcome\n- ADR-0005: JSON column for conversation history (no separate Turn entity)\n\nLet me propose these to the user rather than just creating them.",
