        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Good question. Let me evaluate which Issue #3 decisions meet the ADR criteria (hard to reverse, surprising without context, real trade-off):\n\n**Candidates:**\n\n| Decision | Hard to reverse? | Surprising? | Real trade-off? | ADR? |\n|----------|:---:|:---:|:---:|:----:|\n| Single entity (TriageOutcome as Embeddable, not separate table) | ✅ Schema change | ✅ One might expect a separate result entity | ✅ Simpler vs more flexible | **Yes** |\n| JSON column for conversation (no Turn entity) | ✅ Schema change | ✅ DBAs would normalize | ✅ Queryability vs simplicity | **Yes** |\n| Interface-per-service for test mocking (extracted because final classes) | ❌ Easy to change | ⚠️ Minor | ⚠️ Convention, not architecture | No |\n| TestTriageAnalyzer static double over container mocks | ❌ Testing detail | ❌ Not surprising | ❌ Internal | No |\n| Embeddable nullable columns (Doctrine quirk workaround) | ❌ Tooling detail | ❌ Not wide enough | ❌ Doctrine-specific | No |\n\nTwo worth proposing, both from the grilled session's first decision:\n\n**ADR-0004**: Single Aggregate (TriageSubmission with Embeddable TriageOutcome)\n- Why: Simpler persistence, atomic operations, avoids join for every read\n- Alternative: Separate `triage_outcomes` table with FK back to submissions\n- Trade-off: Can't query outcomes independently, but they're never queried alone\n\n**ADR-0005**: JSON Column for Conversation History\n- Why: Flexible schema, no schema migrations for different message types, perfect for append-only log\n- Alternative: Separate `conversation_turns` table with FK, each turn in its own row\n- Trade-off: Can't SQL-query individual turns, but the app always reads the full history\n\nShall I write these two ADRs?",
